WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:05.440
G'day. Graham Watts. I've looked after these gauges longer than most of

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.200
them have been on the air.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.310
Let me show you round.

4
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.120
These are the stations that measure the rain and the rivers, and

5
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.060
the repeaters that relay their readings home by radio.

6
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:22.610
Here they all are, from the coast to well past the range.

7
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.340
The front page tells you how the network is right now, and

8
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.630
puts the tools you'll reach for most a press away.

9
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.440
Press a station and its card has the lot:

10
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.600
its health, who owns it, and its rain and river neighbours, their

11
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.280
plots a press away.

12
00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:45.610
Every weekday morning the network checks itself, and the crew put what

13
00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.370
needs a look into a short bulletin, with one for the whole

14
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.280
week on Mondays.

15
00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:56.690
On the radio side, it lists every licensed transmitter that could trouble

16
00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.890
a repeater, scored, worst first, with path profiles and coverage on the

17
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:02.360
map.

18
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:06.050
Out in the field it's made for your phone:

19
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:11.210
the stations nearest where you're standing, the inspection sheet, and photos that

20
00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.570
know where they were taken. There's plenty more.

21
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.630
Turbo, you're quicker than me.

22
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.440
Flat out, then! Path profiles, link budgets, radio coverage, repeater sites, ALERT

23
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.830
decoders, bit-flip hunting, interference cases, level surveys, two-peg tests, field photos, printable

24
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.730
path maps, and exports for Radio Mobile and Google Earth!

25
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.600
And the data comes in from the Bureau, ACMA, the Queensland Government,

26
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.000
Transport and Main Roads, the councils' cameras, Geoscience Australia, New South Wales

27
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.310
Spatial Services, the ACCC, OpenStreetMap, Esri and the ECMWF.

28
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.180
Personal best!

29
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:49.030
That's the start of it. The lot is listed on one page,

30
00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.550
a line each, with where each one comes from, and there are

31
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.340
short videos for the jobs you'll do most.

32
00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.910
And it's still growing: new things land all the time, and What's

33
00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:04.350
new, up in the banner, lists each one.

34
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.950
So watch this space. It's going to be a long day, so

35
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.760
we might as well do it properly.
